    "doc": "E-043a \u00b7 Trains a FRESH 40.7M monthly f3 codec on ALL 481 longitude columns: the anchor's exact geometry with the -45..-25 block returned to the stage-1 training pool (recipe f3-anchor-41M-nolonhold, holdout_lon '0,0') \u00b7 params 40.693M \u00b7 stage encoder \u00b7 data family3_na025 (C 39, T 516) \u00b7 arch 576x10, 8 heads, d_dec 768, d_z 64, patch 3 \u00b7 steps x batch 60,000 x 512 (cosine 3e-4 to zero; max_minutes 0, so nothing refits) \u00b7 resume EMPTY \u2014 a fresh codec, there is no no-holdout parent to continue from. CONTROL: f3_anchor41M (run-62/run-63, 40,692,849 params), identical in every architectural field and differing in exactly one, holdout_lon '-45,-25'. HYPOTHESIS: the block cost 25% of the training pixels for regularisation the per-pixel anomaly transform already provides, so this codec should probe at least as well as the anchor on the columns both saw and better overall. FALSIFIER: if its in-training linear r_deseas and its later probe_kfold rapid r sit at or below the anchor's on the shared columns, the holdout was buying real regularisation and the 48%/59% hole-patching decomposition is measuring something else. VERIFY IN THE FIRST MINUTES (ml/CLAUDE.md \u00a72): train.py must print \"NO lon holdout \u2014 all 481 cols train (--holdout-lon '0,0')\" and a train-pixel count ~4/3 of the anchor's. NOTE the anomaly transform's z-score statistics are now taken over all longitudes, so this codec's weight hash and embedding cache are new, not a hit on the anchor's. ONE SEED (Chris 2026-08-19: \"we may not need two runs of everything\"); \u00a73b governs the reading \u2014 this is a new codec regime with no measured pair, so a headline claim buys its replicate before it is quoted.",
